WebSockets
=================
🚀 1. Start with a simple definition
--------------------------------------------
    “WebSocket is a communication protocol that provides full-duplex, real-time
    communication between the client and server over a single, long-lived
    connection.”
    ✅ Full-duplex means both sides (client & server) can send data to each other anytime —
    without needing to make new requests.
💬 2. Compare it with HTTP :
--------------------------------
    “Unlike HTTP, where the client has to send a request every time it needs data,
    WebSocket keeps the connection open. Once the connection is established, the
    server can push updates instantly to the client without waiting for the client to ask.”
    :
1. How HTTP works
-----------------------
    ● HTTP is request–response based.
    ● The client must always start the communication.
    ● Example:
    ○ You open a webpage → your browser (client) sends a request → the server
    sends back a response.
    ● After the response, the connection closes.
    🧠 So, if the client wants new data again (like new messages, orders, notifications), it must
    send another request each time.
    That’s called polling.
⚡ 2. How WebSocket works
---------------------------
    ● WebSocket is full-duplex (two-way) and persistent.
    ● Once a connection is opened, it stays alive until either side closes it.
    ● The server can send data anytime — even without the client asking.
    Example
    ● HTTP → Client must repeatedly ask: “Any updates?”
    ● WebSocket → Server says directly: “Here’s a new message/order/update.”
⚙️ 3. Explain how it works (short & clear)
-------------------------------------------------
    1. The client (e.g., browser) sends an HTTP upgrade request to the server.
    2. The server upgrades the connection from HTTP → WebSocket protocol.
    3. Both sides keep the connection open for continuous communication.
    💡 4. Give a real-world example (this impresses interviewers)
    “In my projects, I used WebSockets to update restaurant dashboards in real-time
    when a new order was placed. So when a customer ordered food, all connected
    restaurant clients received the update instantly — without refreshing the page.”
    🧠 5. (Optional) Mention libraries or tools
    You can say:
    “I used Socket.IO in Node.js, which simplifies WebSocket handling — managing
    connections, rooms, and events for different users.”
    Example snippet:
    io.on('connection', (socket) => {
    console.log('User connected:', socket.id);
    socket.on('newOrder', (data) => {
    io.emit('orderUpdated', data); // send to all clients
    });
    });
    ⚡ 6. End with benefits
    “WebSockets are great for any app needing real-time updates — like chat systems,
    live dashboards, gaming, notifications, or order tracking — because they reduce
    latency and server load compared to frequent HTTP polling.”
    🗣️ Example full answer in interview style:
    “WebSocket is a protocol that enables two-way, real-time communication between
    the client and server. Unlike HTTP, it doesn’t need to open a new connection for
    every request — it keeps one persistent connection open.
    I used it in a food delivery project where restaurant dashboards updated instantly
    whenever a customer placed an order. I implemented it using Socket.IO with
    Node.js — the server emitted an event for a new order, and all connected clients
    received it in real-time.
    It’s mainly useful for apps needing instant updates like chat, notifications, or live
    tracking.”
